"The surah begins with the expression \"Subhan\". This word means to declare Allah free and exalted above any imperfection. Commentators have stated that tasbih is a verbal noun and expresses the sanctity of Allah's essence. Furthermore, this expression is also used in moments of astonishment and helplessness. The hadiths concerning the splendors (subuhat) of Allah's Countenance show that this concept points to the manifestations of divinity.\n\nThere are several wisdoms behind the surah beginning with this tasbih: First, to glorify the astonishing event of the Isra (the Night Journey) and to prepare hearts for it. Second, to proclaim the greatness of Allah's power and forgiveness. Third, to draw attention to the importance of declaring Allah's transcendence (tanzih) in connection with the destruction of the al-Aqsa Mosque mentioned below. Fourth, to point to the connection between the overall theme of the surah and Allah's perfection.\n\nThen, Allah informs that He carried His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque (al-Masjid al-Haram) to the Farthest Mosque (al-Masjid al-Aqsa). The al-Aqsa Mosque is Bayt al-Maqdis in Jerusalem; its surroundings have been endowed with religious and worldly blessings. The purpose of this journey was to show the Prophet His great signs. Following the Isra, the Mir'aj (Ascension) took place; the Prophet was taken to Bayt al-Maqdis upon Buraq, and from there he was ascended to the heavens via the Mir'aj. In each of the heavens, he met with prophets, saw Paradise and Hell, and reached the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary (Sidrat al-Muntaha).\n\nIt is reported that during the Mir'aj, the prayer was made obligatory for the Prophet. The prayer, which was initially commanded as fifty times a day, was reduced to five times through appeals made upon the recommendation of Moses (Musa). However, it was conveyed that these five daily prayers would be counted as fifty in terms of reward. In addition, the Prophet witnessed various scenes in the heavens; he observed the punishments of sinners such as those who consume usury and those who commit adultery.\n\nWhen the Prophet returned to the Sacred Mosque in the morning and informed Quraysh of this event, the polytheists were astonished, and some believers apostatized. However, Abu Bakr affirmed the Prophet and received the title \"al-Siddiq\" (the Truthful). The polytheists asked questions about Bayt al-Maqdis and the caravan; even though the answers given by the Prophet turned out to be correct, they still did not believe. The miracle of Isra is a divine sign that surpasses the measures of human reason; yet indications such as Buraq traveling at the speed of lightning open a door for contemplation.\n\nFollowing the account of the Isra, the surah addresses the giving of the Scripture to Moses and the warnings directed at the Children of Israel. Allah made the Torah a guide for the Children of Israel and required them not to take any trustee besides Him. Then, addressing the descendants of those carried with Noah, it is recalled that he was a grateful servant, and they are warned against ingratitude.\n\nIt was revealed to the Children of Israel in the Torah that they would cause corruption on earth twice and rise to great arrogance. At the time of the first corruption, mighty servants were sent against them, who entered Bayt al-Maqdis and wrought destruction. Afterwards, the Children of Israel were granted victory over them once more and aided with wealth and children. At the time of the second corruption, enemies would be sent again, who would bring sorrow to their faces and destroy the mosque. Nonetheless, it is hoped that their Lord will have mercy; but if they return to spreading corruption, Allah will also return to punishing them. Hell has been prepared as an encompassing prison for the disbelievers.\n\nAllah had informed the Children of Israel in the Scripture that they would cause corruption twice and display great tyranny. When the time for the punishment of the first corruption arrived, upon their killing of Isaiah (peace be upon him), Allah set His mighty and warrior servants (Nebuchadnezzar) over them. These servants searched through the homes and slaughtered the Children of Israel, and this became the promised retribution. This event is illustrated with a parable given by Allah: A piece of land with an owner was developed with a sturdy mansion, a river, and trees; yet despite the watchman, when the trees yielded wild carob pods, its owners willed the demolition of the land. In this parable, the wall represents Allah's protection, the mansion the Sharia, the river the Scripture, the watchman the prophet, the trees the Children of Israel, and the carob pods their evil deeds. Allah judged them according to the judgment they had passed against themselves.\n\nWhen the time for the punishment of the second corruption arrived, upon their killing of John (peace be upon him) and their attempt to kill Jesus (peace be upon him), the Romans were sent to disgrace their faces, enter the Mosque (Jerusalem), and destroy everything completely. This can be considered the outcome of three tragedies. Allah stated that if you do good, you do good for yourselves, and if you do evil, it is against yourselves. After the first punishment, the Children of Israel repented and reformed, and their kingdom was restored to them; but when their second corruption began, the final punishment arrived.\n\nThen, Allah stated: It may be that your Lord will have mercy upon you if you take the path of reform; but if you return, We shall return—meaning, We will punish you again. Thereafter, the glad tidings were given that the Quran guides to what is most upright, that there is a great reward for the believers who do righteous deeds, and that a painful punishment has been prepared for those who do not believe in the Hereafter. Indeed, this mercy was realized through the sending of the Prophet of the end of times.\n\nIt is stated that man is hasty, praying for evil just as he prays for good. Allah has made the night and the day two signs (proofs); He erased the sign of the night and made the sign of the day illuminating, so that you may seek bounty from your Lord and know the number of years. Every person's record of deeds is fastened to their neck, and on the Day of Resurrection, a book will be brought forth which they will find wide open, and it will be said: 'Read your book; today your own soul suffices as an accountant against you.' Whoever is guided is only guided for himself, and whoever goes astray only goes astray against himself; no bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another, and We never punish until We have sent a messenger.\n\nWhen We wish to destroy a city, We command its affluent elite; but they disobey and commit wickedness, so the word is justified against it and it is utterly destroyed. How many generations have been destroyed in this manner after Noah! Whoever desires the immediate world, We hasten for them therein whatever We will, then We prepare Hell for them. But whoever desires the Hereafter and strives for it while being a believer, their striving will be appreciated. The bounty of your Lord is not restricted. The Hereafter is greater in degrees and greater in distinction. Do not set up another deity with Allah, lest you sit condemned and forsaken.\n\nAlmighty Allah informs that He has made the night and the day two signs, erasing the sign of the night and making the sign of the day illuminating. It is understood that the moon was previously illuminating and warming like the sun, and was later effaced and extinguished. As a trace of this effacement, the dark markings on the surface of the moon and the phases of its light are pointed out. According to narrations, Abdullah ibn Salam asked the Prophet about the dark markings on the moon, and referring to the verse 'We erased the sign of the night,' he stated that this was the erasure. This event serves as an example of occurrences such as the folding up and extinguishing of the sun on the Day of Resurrection, and is also significant from a scientific perspective. Allah made the distinction between night and day so that people might seek bounty from their Lord and know the number of years and reckoning.\n\nThe verses stating that every human being's deeds are tied to their neck, that on the Day of Resurrection they will encounter an open book, and that it will be said, 'Read your book; today you yourself are sufficient to take account of yourself,' call humanity to responsibility. In a noble hadith, it is also said: 'Take account of yourselves before you are called to account.' Whoever follows the right path does so for his own good, and whoever goes astray does so only to his own detriment. Allah does not punish anyone until He sends a messenger; guidance and error are made known through messengers, and whether to accept or reject is left to the individual's own free will.\n\nWhen We intend to destroy a city, We command its affluent ones to obey, but they commit evil deeds therein; thus, they justly incur the punishment. Whoever desires the transient worldly life, We give him promptly whatever We wish, and then We prepare Hell for him. But whoever desires the Hereafter, strives for it as it should be striven for, and is a believer, their striving will be appreciated. Allah bestows His blessings upon both groups; those who strive for the world are not deprived in this world, and those who strive for the Hereafter can also benefit from worldly blessings.\n\nSee how We have favored some people over others; yet the ranks of the Hereafter are greater and higher in distinction. O human being! Do not invent another deity alongside Allah, lest you find yourself condemned and forsaken. Associating partners with Allah is the greatest injustice. Tawhid (monotheism) is the first condition of faith and the foundation of morality and action. Allah commands kindness to parents, giving relatives, the needy, and the traveler their due, not squandering wealth, not approaching adultery, not taking life unjustly, not approaching the orphan's property, fulfilling pledges, honesty in measure and weight, not pursuing that of which one has no knowledge, and not acting arrogantly.\n\nThese are among the wisdoms your Lord has revealed to you. Do not invent another deity alongside Allah, or you will be cast into Hell. The polytheists utter a monstrous claim by saying that Allah has singled out sons for Himself and taken daughters from among the angels. Various warnings have been explained in the Quran, but this has only increased their aversion. Say: 'If there were other deities alongside Allah as they claim, they would certainly have sought a way to the Lord of the Throne.' Allah is transcendent and exalted far above what they say. The seven heavens, the earth, and all who are in them glorify Allah; yet you do not understand their glorification. Indeed, He is Forbearing, Most Forgiving.\n\nWhen the Quran is recited, a veil is drawn between you and those who do not believe in the Hereafter; coverings are placed over their hearts and heaviness in their ears. When your Lord is mentioned as One, they turn their backs and flee in aversion. They whisper to one another while listening to you, saying: 'You are only following a bewitched man.' Look at the comparisons they make for you, and so they have fallen into error. They say, 'When we have become bones and dust, will we really be resurrected?' Say: 'Be you stones or iron, or any other creation that seems impossible in your minds, you will certainly be resurrected.' The One who created you the first time will resurrect you. On a near day, Allah will summon you, you will respond by rushing to Him, and you will think that you stayed only a short while.\n\nTell My believing servants to speak the best words; Satan sows discord among them. Your Lord knows you best; if He wills, He will have mercy on you through your repentance, or if He wills, He will punish you. We have favored some prophets over others; and We gave David the Psalms (Zabur). This refutes the Jews' claim that 'there is no prophet after Moses (Musa).' Those whom the polytheists worship seek a means of nearness to their Lord; they hope for His mercy and fear His punishment. There is no city but that We will destroy it before the Day of Resurrection or punish it with severe torment. This is inscribed in the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawh al-Mahfuz).\n\nWhat prevented Us from sending signs is that the earlier peoples denied them. We gave the people of Thamud the she-camel, but they wronged it. We send signs only to frighten and warn. The sights We showed you on the night of the Ascension (Mi'raj) and the cursed tree mentioned in the Quran are a trial for the people. We frighten them, but it only increases them in greater transgression. Your Lord encompasses humanity; He knows everyone through His knowledge.\n\nIn the Quran, the cursed tree refers to the Zaqqum tree. This tree was made a means of trial (fitnah) for the polytheists. Indeed, in Surahs as-Saffat, ad-Dukhan, and al-Waqi'ah, it is explained that the Zaqqum tree is the food of torment in Hell. Polytheists such as Abu Jahl and Ibn al-Ziba'ra mocked the Zaqqum by eating dates and clotted cream, thus deceiving the public. However, Allah's power is fully capable of making a tree grow in the midst of fire. These verses drove the obstinate even further into curse and transgression.\n\nWhen the angels were commanded to prostrate to Adam, they all prostrated except Iblis, who arrogantly refused. Iblis asked Allah for respite to mislead most of Adam's descendants; Allah granted him permission, but declared that he has no authority over sincere servants. To deceive humanity, Satan makes promises with his cavalry and infantry forces, becoming a partner in wealth and children, but his promise is nothing more than deception.\n\nAllah has granted people the ability to seek His bounty by making ships sail across the sea. When a disaster strikes at sea, people forget everyone other than Allah, but when He delivers them to safety, they turn away. Indeed, man is ungrateful. One cannot feel safe against being swallowed up by the earth on land or being struck by a violent sandstorm. The children of Adam have been honored, carried on land and sea, provided with pure provisions, and favored above many of the created beings.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, every community of people will be summoned with their leaders. Whoever is given their record in their right hand will read it without suffering any injustice. Whoever is spiritually blind in this world will also be blind in the Hereafter. The Prophet was pressured to utter something other than revelation, but Allah granted him steadfastness. The disbelievers attempted to drive the Prophet out of his homeland, but they were able to remain only for a short time. This is also the established way of Allah (Sunnah) regarding previous prophets.\n\nPrayer should be performed from the declining of the sun until the darkness of the night (noon, afternoon, evening, and night prayers), along with the dawn prayer. The angels of the night and day witness the dawn prayer. The night prayer (tahajjud) was commanded as an extra voluntary (nafilah) prayer specifically for the Prophet, and he was given the glad tidings that he would attain the Praised Station (al-Maqam al-Mahmud) through it. When truth arrives, falsehood perishes. The Quran is a healing and mercy for the believers; for the wrongdoers, it increases only loss. Everyone acts according to their own disposition and intent, and Allah knows best who is on the right path.\n\nTo those who ask about the soul (ruh), it was said: The soul is of the command of my Lord, and you have been given but little knowledge. There are different narrations regarding the occasion of revelation of this verse; it is said to have been revealed in Mecca or in Medina. The nature of the soul is examined from three perspectives: as the origin of motion, life, and perception. The word 'command' (amr) denotes creation through Allah's command 'Be.' The knowledge given to humans is limited; the true reality of the soul is known only to Allah.\n\nIn response to what the Prophet (peace be upon him) told them regarding knowledge, when those who asked, \"Is this address directed to us?\" were told by the Prophet, \"Nay, both us and you,\" they were astonished and said, \"At times you say, 'Whoever is granted wisdom has indeed been granted abundant good,' and at other times you say this. Even though you recite from the Quran that the Torah was given to us, how can you say that the knowledge given to us is little?\" The Messenger of Allah replied, \"It is little compared to the knowledge of Allah. Allah has given you so much of it that if you act upon it, you will benefit,\" and for this reason the verse, \"If all the trees on the earth were pens and the sea were ink...\" was revealed. This address is not part of the previous command, but a general address that begins anew. All of human knowledge is little compared to the knowledge of Allah; it is limited and transient. Allah's knowledge, on the other hand, belongs to His essence, encompasses all things, and is infinite. The entirety of knowledge is only possible through knowing Allah's essence, which only He knows.\n\nThe important points here are as follows: 1) The verse, \"Allah taught Adam all the names,\" does not mean that the reality of everything having a name was taught; this, too, is little knowledge compared to Allah's knowledge. 2) Verses such as \"...to explain all things\" encompass everything that human beings need in terms of religion and legal rulings; that is, it is relative, not absolute. 3) The deficiency of human knowledge is not limited solely to knowing the spirit (ruh); our other knowledge is also little because it is based on the spirit. 4) In this address, there is an honor that grants humanity a share of knowledge, and at the same time, a blow that shatters intellectual pride. In the face of the infinity of Allah's knowledge, man must be humble and realize that the possibility of progress in knowledge is endless, yet must never become arrogant. It should not be forgotten that the knowledge granted to man can also be taken away by Allah.\n\nWhile Allah explains that the knowledge given to His Prophet is exempted from being little, He expresses this truth above all with an oath and emphasis: \"And if We willed, We could surely take away that which We have revealed to you.\" That is, We could even erase from memories the Quran that We sent down with the Holy Spirit (Ruh al-Qudus). Then you would not find for yourself against Us any helper. Except as a mercy from your Lord. Indeed, your Lord's favor upon you is very great. This warning is very important to avoid pride.\n\nBy oath, even if all humans and jinn were to come together to produce the like of this Quran, even if they backed each other up, they could not produce the like of it. The Quran is such a great miracle. The reason for the revelation of this verse is as follows: A group from among the Jews objected, not seeing the Quran ordered like the Torah, and the Prophet stated that they knew it was from Allah's presence. When they said, \"We too can bring you the like of it,\" this verse was revealed. A group from Quraysh also made a similar claim. Ever since that day, despite all attempts, this verse has proven its truth.\n\nThe pretext of those who do not believe in the Prophet is this: \"Has Allah sent a human being as a messenger?\" If there were angels walking on earth, an angel messenger would have been sent down to them from the heaven. Allah is sufficient as a witness. Whomever Allah guides is rightly guided. On the Day of Resurrection, the disbelievers will be gathered on their faces—blind, deaf, and mute—and will suffer torment in Hell. They do not believe that they will be resurrected after becoming bones and dust. They do not realize that Allah has the power to create them anew. Even if man possessed the treasures of his Lord's mercy, he would still be stingy. Allah gave Moses (Musa) nine clear signs; Pharaoh thought he was bewitched, while Moses knew him to be doomed to destruction. Pharaoh wanted to drive Moses and the Children of Israel out of the land, but Allah drowned him and those with him, then commanded the Children of Israel to dwell in that land, and announced that He would gather them all together on the Day of Resurrection.\n\nWe have sent down this Quran in truth, and with truth it has descended. We have sent you only as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner. We have revealed the Quran in sections so that you may recite it to the people at a deliberate pace. Those who were given knowledge before it fall down in prostration when the Quran is recited, and say, \"Glory be to our Lord, His promise has been fulfilled.\" They fall down in prostration weeping, and it increases their humility. Say: \"Call upon Allah or call upon the Most Merciful (al-Rahman); whichever name you call upon, to Him belong the most beautiful names.\" Neither raise your voice too loud in your prayer nor recite it too softly, but seek a middle course. Praise be to Allah; He has not taken a son, He has no partner, and He has no need of an ally out of weakness. Magnify Him with magnification (takbir). This verse establishes a beautiful harmony between the tasbih (glorification) at the beginning of the surah and the hamd (praise) and takbir (magnification) at its end."